NNAAMMEE
       ipcs - show information on IPC facilities

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       iippccss [options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       iippccss shows information on System V inter-process communication
       facilities. By default it shows information about all three resources:
       shared memory segments, message queues, and semaphore arrays.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ii, ----iidd _i_d
           Show full details on just the one resource element identified by
           _i_d. This option needs to be combined with one of the three resource
           options: --mm, --qq or --ss.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

   RReessoouurrccee ooppttiioonnss
       --mm, ----sshhmmeemmss
           Write information about active shared memory segments.

       --qq, ----qquueeuueess
           Write information about active message queues.

       --ss, ----sseemmaapphhoorreess
           Write information about active semaphore sets.

       --aa, ----aallll
           Write information about all three resources (default).

   OOuuttppuutt ffoorrmmaattss
       Of these options only one takes effect: the last one specified.

       --cc, ----ccrreeaattoorr
           Show creator and owner.

       --ll, ----lliimmiittss
           Show resource limits.

       --pp, ----ppiidd
           Show PIDs of creator and last operator.

       --tt, ----ttiimmee
           Write time information. The time of the last control operation that
           changed the access permissions for all facilities, the time of the
           last mmssggssnndd(2) and mmssggrrccvv(2) operations on message queues, the time
           of the last sshhmmaatt(2) and sshhmmddtt(2) operations on shared memory, and
           the time of the last sseemmoopp(2) operation on semaphores.

       --uu, ----ssuummmmaarryy
           Show status summary.

   RReepprreesseennttaattiioonn
       These affect only the --ll (----lliimmiittss) option.

       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print the sizes in bytes rather than in a human-readable format.

           By default, the unit, sizes are expressed in, is byte, and unit
           prefixes are in power of 2^10 (1024). Abbreviations of symbols are
           exhibited truncated in order to reach a better readability, by
           exhibiting alone the first letter of them; examples: "1 KiB" and "1
           MiB" are respectively exhibited as "1 K" and "1 M", then omitting
           on purpose the mention "iB", which is part of these abbreviations.

       ----hhuummaann
           Print sizes in human-readable format.

CCOONNFFOORRMMIINNGG TTOO
       The Linux iippccss utility is not fully compatible to the POSIX iippccss
       utility. The Linux version does not support the POSIX --aa, --bb and --oo
       options, but does support the --ll and --uu options not defined by POSIX. A
       portable application shall not use the --aa, --bb, --oo, --ll, and --uu options.

NNOOTTEESS
       The current implementation of iippccss obtains information about available
       IPC resources by parsing the files in _/_p_r_o_c_/_s_y_s_v_i_p_c. Before util-linux
       version v2.23, an alternate mechanism was used: the IIPPCC__SSTTAATT command of
       mmssggccttll(2), sseemmccttll(2), and sshhmmccttll(2). This mechanism is also used in
       later util-linux versions in the case where _/_p_r_o_c is unavailable. A
       limitation of the IIPPCC__SSTTAATT mechanism is that it can only be used to
       retrieve information about IPC resources for which the user has read
       permission.

